10	{"ts": "2026-08-09T11:02:00Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "query_metric", "detail": "sum(k6_http_req_duration_milliseconds_count) and sum(k6_http_req_failed_total) over 6h/4h step=600 -- initially summed across ALL load-generator request URLs", "reasoning": "Checking whether the load-generator itself was still issuing real HTTP requests at all. Counter reset at ~07:19Z (consistent with a load-generator container restart), then climbed steadily afterward including right through the 09:56Z 'stop' point -- at first glance this looked like traffic never stopped, which would have contradicted every metric above, so it needed decomposing by URL before trusting it."}
11	{"ts": "2026-08-09T11:03:00Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "disconfirm", "detail": "sum(k6_http_reqs_total{url=~\"http://frontend-proxy.*\"}) vs sum(k6_http_reqs_total{url=~\"http://flagd.*\"}), 5-min steps over last 2h", "reasoning": "Split the load-generator's own request counter by destination to check whether the continued growth above was real app traffic or just its internal housekeeping. frontend-proxy-bound requests flatlined at 6032 from 09:56Z onward (zero growth for 65+ minutes); flagd-bound requests (its own feature-flag polling) kept climbing the entire time. This resolved the apparent contradiction: the load-generator process is alive and busy, but its scripted app-traffic scenario specifically has stopped."}
12	{"ts": "2026-08-09T11:04:00Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "query_flags", "detail": "curl http://10.10.1.141:4001/list re-check, specifically loadGeneratorTraffic and loadGeneratorVUs", "reasoning": "Per playbook correlate-with-flag-changes: loadGeneratorTraffic's own description is 'Turn off to pause all load-generator scenarios' and its current defaultVariant is 'off' -- directly matches the observed behavior (VUs alive, flag-polling alive, app-scenario dead). Treating this as a strong causal lead, not yet proof, since /list only shows the current defaultVariant, not when it changed."}
