14	{"ts": "2026-08-09T11:06:00Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "disconfirm", "detail": "Loki: error/exception/fail/timeout/unavailable filter against product-catalog and checkout logs, 07:20Z-09:00Z (the escalating-error window seen from frontend's client-side spans)", "reasoning": "Before concluding the 07:27-08:57 error spike was a transport-level failure rather than a backend application bug, checked whether product-catalog or checkout logged any internal errors during that exact window. Zero lines from either service -- the errors frontend observed never reached these services' application code, which is what you'd expect from a load-generator/network-level disruption rather than a genuine backend fault."}
15	{"ts": "2026-08-09T11:07:00Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "disconfirm", "detail": "Direct read-only GET http://10.10.1.141:8080/ and GET http://10.10.1.141:8080/api/products/0PUK6V6EV0, then immediately re-checked product-catalog's rpc_server_call_duration_seconds_count{rpc_response_status_code=\"OK\"} for GetProduct", "reasoning": "The strongest possible disconfirming check for 'is the app actually broken': bypass the load-generator entirely and hit the storefront myself. Both requests returned 200 in under 120ms, and the product-page request visibly bumped product-catalog's own OK counter by exactly 1 immediately afterward -- direct, first-hand proof that frontend-proxy, frontend, and product-catalog are correctly wired and healthy right now. This is why the alert's severity stayed at medium rather than high/critical: there is no live user-impact, only a real, unexplained traffic blackout and an unresolved precursor error spike."}
16	{"ts": "2026-08-09T11:08:00Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "raise_alert", "detail": "Wrote alerts/alert-001.json: 'Load-generator's synthetic traffic to the storefront has been completely dark for over an hour after a ~90min error spike; app itself verified healthy by direct probe.' severity=medium, confidence=0.8", "reasoning": "Raising because this is a real, multiply-corroborated anomaly (flag state, log timestamp, and independent metric flatlines across 5+ services all agree on timing) with a genuine unresolved question (what caused the 07:27-08:57 error spike, and why hasn't loadGeneratorTraffic been turned back on) -- not raising it would mean staying silent about an hour-long observability blind spot in the only monitored environment I have. Kept severity at medium, not high, because the disconfirming direct-probe check proved there is no actual user-facing breakage right now."}
